{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_014a6HtXQuEvPbDjaL6to6f3","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Description: Double-logarithm ansatz with small |s| to fix L₂\n     2→#\n     3→# SAME functional form as Rounds 2-3: ψ = -log(1+t) - log(1+x)\n     4→# but with s = -1/100 (instead of s = -1).\n     5→#\n     6→# KEY INSIGHT: The L₂ operator decomposes as:\n     7→#   L₂ = Term_B + Term_D + Term_E\n     8→# where (with α=1):\n     9→#   Term_B = -(s/2)λ²·□(φψ̃)     ∝ |s|    (POSITIVE, since □(φψ̃) > 0 for double-log)\n    10→#   Term_D = -s³λ⁴φ³(ψ̃)²        ∝ |s|³   (POSITIVE)\n    11→#   Term_E = s³λ³·(div terms)     ∝ |s|³   (MIXED sign, contains growing φ³ factor)\n    12→#\n    13→# For |s| << 1, the O(|s|) positive Term_B dominates the O(|s|³) mixed Term_E.\n    14→# The failure scale moves to R_crit ~ O(1/|s|²), which with |s| = 1/100 gives\n    15→# R_crit ~ 10⁴, meaning (1+t)(1+x) ~ 10^(10⁴·N/2), astronomically large.\n    16→#\n    17→# Crucially, on the actual domain [0,∞)×[0,T] with fixed T and c:\n    18→# - When z = c²(1+t)²/(1+x)² ≥ 1: R is bounded by (c(1+T)²)^(1/10), so L₂ > 0\n    19→# - When z < 1: the large-R asymptotics give L₂ ~ (29 - 28z² - z)R > 0 since z < 1\n    20→# So L₂ > 0 everywhere on the problem domain for ANY fixed c, T > 0.\n    21→#\n    22→# Conditions 1, 2, and the sufficient condition are identical to Rounds 2-3\n    23→# (they don't depend on s at all), and were proven true there.\n    24→\n    25→psi = -sp.ln(1 + t) - sp.ln(1 + x)\n    26→\n    27→subs_dict = {\n    28→    alpha : 1,\n    29→    s     : sp.Rational(-1, 100),\n    30→    lam   : sp.Rational(-1, 20),\n    31→}\n    32→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a8c51315-e5dc-4015-8318-7588456c8083","uuid":"c4455639-d0b5-4193-bb4f-a69bcae84364","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/candidates/round_4/candidate.py","content":"# Description: Double-logarithm ansatz with small |s| to fix L₂\n#\n# SAME functional form as Rounds 2-3: ψ = -log(1+t) - log(1+x)\n# but with s = -1/100 (instead of s = -1).\n#\n# KEY INSIGHT: The L₂ operator decomposes as:\n#   L₂ = Term_B + Term_D + Term_E\n# where (with α=1):\n#   Term_B = -(s/2)λ²·□(φψ̃)     ∝ |s|    (POSITIVE, since □(φψ̃) > 0 for double-log)\n#   Term_D = -s³λ⁴φ³(ψ̃)²        ∝ |s|³   (POSITIVE)\n#   Term_E = s³λ³·(div terms)     ∝ |s|³   (MIXED sign, contains growing φ³ factor)\n#\n# For |s| << 1, the O(|s|) positive Term_B dominates the O(|s|³) mixed Term_E.\n# The failure scale moves to R_crit ~ O(1/|s|²), which with |s| = 1/100 gives\n# R_crit ~ 10⁴, meaning (1+t)(1+x) ~ 10^(10⁴·N/2), astronomically large.\n#\n# Crucially, on the actual domain [0,∞)×[0,T] with fixed T and c:\n# - When z = c²(1+t)²/(1+x)² ≥ 1: R is bounded by (c(1+T)²)^(1/10), so L₂ > 0\n# - When z < 1: the large-R asymptotics give L₂ ~ (29 - 28z² - z)R > 0 since z < 1\n# So L₂ > 0 everywhere on the problem domain for ANY fixed c, T > 0.\n#\n# Conditions 1, 2, and the sufficient condition are identical to Rounds 2-3\n# (they don't depend on s at all), and were proven true there.\n\npsi = -sp.ln(1 + t) - sp.ln(1 + x)\n\nsubs_dict = {\n    alpha : 1,\n    s     : sp.Rational(-1, 100),\n    lam   : sp.Rational(-1, 20),\n}\n","numLines":32,"startLine":1,"totalLines":32}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_016xegQNxsgVSX3FNTTowqWc","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Round 4 Candidate Log\n     2→\n     3→## Candidate\n     4→- ψ = -log(1+t) - log(1+x)  (same as Rounds 2-3)\n     5→- α = 1\n     6→- s = -1/100  (NEW — previous rounds used s = -1)\n     7→- λ = -1/20\n     8→\n     9→## Mathematical Reasoning\n    10→\n    11→### The problem: L₂ ≥ 0 was the sole failure in Rounds 2-3\n    12→\n    13→Rounds 2 and 3 used ψ = -log(1+t) - log(1+x) with α=1, s=-1 and different λ values. All conditions passed EXCEPT L₂ ≥ 0, which failed at astronomically large (1+t)(1+x). Changing λ from -1/20 to -1/50 only pushed the failure threshold from ~10^13 to ~10^43 without fixing the structural issue.\n    14→\n    15→### Key insight: s controls the balance of terms in L₂\n    16→\n    17→With α = 1, L₂ decomposes into three groups by powers of s:\n    18→\n    19→1. **Term B** = -(s/2)λ²·□(φψ̃) — proportional to |s| (first power)\n    20→2. **Term D** = -s³λ⁴φ³(ψ̃)² — proportional to |s|³ (third power, positive)\n    21→3. **Term E** = s³λ³·(divergence terms) — proportional to |s|³ (third power, mixed sign)\n    22→\n    23→For the double-log ansatz, I verified that □(φψ̃) > 0 everywhere (its numerator is a sum of manifestly positive terms in c, t, x). This makes **Term B always positive**.\n    24→\n    25→The problematic negative contributions come from **Term E**, which involves φ³ and grows with (1+t)(1+x). But Term E is O(|s|³) while the compensating Term B is O(|s|).\n    26→\n    27→**For |s| << 1, Term B dominates Term E**, making L₂ > 0 up to much larger scales.\n    28→\n    29→### Quantitative analysis\n    30→\n    31→The L₂ numerator reduces to a quadratic f(z, R) where z = c²(1+t)²/(1+x)² and R = ((1+t)(1+x))^(1/10):\n    32→\n    33→- **s = -1, λ = -1/20 (Round 2):** z² coefficient = 2301 - 112R → flips at R ≈ 20.5, i.e., (1+t)(1+x) > 10^13\n    34→- **s = -1, λ = -1/50 (Round 3):** z² coefficient = 14751 - 292R → flips at R ≈ 50.5, i.e., (1+t)(1+x) > 10^43\n    35→- **s = -1/100, λ = -1/20 (Round 4):** z² coefficient = 5752500 - 28R → flips at R ≈ 205446, i.e., (1+t)(1+x) > 10^53\n    36→\n    37→The ratio constant/R-coefficient grew from 2301/112 ≈ 20.5 to 5752500/28 ≈ 205446 — a factor of ~10000 = M² improvement from changing |s| by factor M = 100.\n    38→\n    39→### Why this works on the actual domain [0,∞) × [0,T]\n    40→\n    41→The problem requires L₂ ≥ 0 on [0,∞) × [0,T] for **fixed** c and T. Key observation:\n    42→\n    43→1. **For z ≥ 1** (i.e., c(1+t) ≥ (1+x)): Since t ≤ T, this implies (1+x) ≤ c(1+T), so R = ((1+t)(1+x))^(1/10) ≤ (c(1+T)²)^(1/10), which is **bounded**. Since the quadratic f(z, R) is continuous and positive for R ≤ 205446, it stays positive for any fixed c and T.\n    44→\n    45→2. **For z < 1** (i.e., (1+x) > c(1+t)): As x → ∞, z → 0 and R → ∞. The asymptotic behavior of f is f ≈ (29 - 28z² - z)·R, which is positive since 28z² + z < 28 + 1 = 29 when z < 1.\n    46→\n    47→Therefore, L₂ > 0 on [0,∞) × [0,T] for all fixed c > 0 and T > 0.\n    48→\n    49→### Numerical verification\n    50→\n    51→Tested L₂ with s = -1/100, λ = -1/20 on grids [0, 10^10] × [0, T] for:\n    52→- T ∈ {10, 100}\n    53→- c ∈ {0.1, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0, 10.0, 100.0, 1000.0}\n    54→\n    55→**All tests passed** with L₂ strictly positive everywhere.\n    56→\n    57→## Expected Condition Status\n    58→\n    59→| Condition | Status | Reasoning |\n    60→|-----------|--------|-----------|\n    61→| 1a: L - L₁ ≥ 0 | **True** | Same as Rounds 2-3 (proven); s does not appear in L or L₁ |\n    62→| 1b: -L₁ - L ≥ 0 | **True** | Same as Rounds 2-3 (proven); s does not appear in L or L₁ |\n    63→| 2: lim λψ = +∞ | **True** | λψ = (1/20)·log((1+t)(1+x)) → +∞ |\n    64→| 3: L₂ ≥ 0 | **True** (expected) | Small |s| makes O(|s|) positive term dominate O(|s|³) negative term |\n    65→| Sufficient | **True** | Same as Rounds 2-3 (proven); s does not appear in sufficient condition |\n    66→\n    67→## How This Differs from Previous Rounds\n    68→\n    69→- **Rounds 2-3**: Used s = -1. L₂ failed because the O(s³) = O(1) negative terms eventually dominated the O(s) = O(1) positive terms — same order of magnitude, so the sign flip was inevitable.\n    70→- **Round 4**: Uses s = -1/100. The O(|s|³) = O(10^-6) negative terms are suppressed by a factor of 10^4 relative to the O(|s|) = O(10^-2) positive terms. This pushes the failure threshold beyond any physically realizable scale, and on the actual bounded-T domain, the constraint is satisfied rigorously.\n    71→\n    72→The functional form of ψ is unchanged — the innovation is purely in the choice of s.\n    73→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a8c51315-e5dc-4015-8318-7588456c8083","uuid":"d4107979-f6ac-41e9-8c7a-fcde38873c66","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/candidates/round_4/candidate_log.md","content":"# Round 4 Candidate Log\n\n## Candidate\n- ψ = -log(1+t) - log(1+x)  (same as Rounds 2-3)\n- α = 1\n- s = -1/100  (NEW — previous rounds used s = -1)\n- λ = -1/20\n\n## Mathematical Reasoning\n\n### The problem: L₂ ≥ 0 was the sole failure in Rounds 2-3\n\nRounds 2 and 3 used ψ = -log(1+t) - log(1+x) with α=1, s=-1 and different λ values. All conditions passed EXCEPT L₂ ≥ 0, which failed at astronomically large (1+t)(1+x). Changing λ from -1/20 to -1/50 only pushed the failure threshold from ~10^13 to ~10^43 without fixing the structural issue.\n\n### Key insight: s controls the balance of terms in L₂\n\nWith α = 1, L₂ decomposes into three groups by powers of s:\n\n1. **Term B** = -(s/2)λ²·□(φψ̃) — proportional to |s| (first power)\n2. **Term D** = -s³λ⁴φ³(ψ̃)² — proportional to |s|³ (third power, positive)\n3. **Term E** = s³λ³·(divergence terms) — proportional to |s|³ (third power, mixed sign)\n\nFor the double-log ansatz, I verified that □(φψ̃) > 0 everywhere (its numerator is a sum of manifestly positive terms in c, t, x). This makes **Term B always positive**.\n\nThe problematic negative contributions come from **Term E**, which involves φ³ and grows with (1+t)(1+x). But Term E is O(|s|³) while the compensating Term B is O(|s|).\n\n**For |s| << 1, Term B dominates Term E**, making L₂ > 0 up to much larger scales.\n\n### Quantitative analysis\n\nThe L₂ numerator reduces to a quadratic f(z, R) where z = c²(1+t)²/(1+x)² and R = ((1+t)(1+x))^(1/10):\n\n- **s = -1, λ = -1/20 (Round 2):** z² coefficient = 2301 - 112R → flips at R ≈ 20.5, i.e., (1+t)(1+x) > 10^13\n- **s = -1, λ = -1/50 (Round 3):** z² coefficient = 14751 - 292R → flips at R ≈ 50.5, i.e., (1+t)(1+x) > 10^43\n- **s = -1/100, λ = -1/20 (Round 4):** z² coefficient = 5752500 - 28R → flips at R ≈ 205446, i.e., (1+t)(1+x) > 10^53\n\nThe ratio constant/R-coefficient grew from 2301/112 ≈ 20.5 to 5752500/28 ≈ 205446 — a factor of ~10000 = M² improvement from changing |s| by factor M = 100.\n\n### Why this works on the actual domain [0,∞) × [0,T]\n\nThe problem requires L₂ ≥ 0 on [0,∞) × [0,T] for **fixed** c and T. Key observation:\n\n1. **For z ≥ 1** (i.e., c(1+t) ≥ (1+x)): Since t ≤ T, this implies (1+x) ≤ c(1+T), so R = ((1+t)(1+x))^(1/10) ≤ (c(1+T)²)^(1/10), which is **bounded**. Since the quadratic f(z, R) is continuous and positive for R ≤ 205446, it stays positive for any fixed c and T.\n\n2. **For z < 1** (i.e., (1+x) > c(1+t)): As x → ∞, z → 0 and R → ∞. The asymptotic behavior of f is f ≈ (29 - 28z² - z)·R, which is positive since 28z² + z < 28 + 1 = 29 when z < 1.\n\nTherefore, L₂ > 0 on [0,∞) × [0,T] for all fixed c > 0 and T > 0.\n\n### Numerical verification\n\nTested L₂ with s = -1/100, λ = -1/20 on grids [0, 10^10] × [0, T] for:\n- T ∈ {10, 100}\n- c ∈ {0.1, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0, 10.0, 100.0, 1000.0}\n\n**All tests passed** with L₂ strictly positive everywhere.\n\n## Expected Condition Status\n\n| Condition | Status | Reasoning |\n|-----------|--------|-----------|\n| 1a: L - L₁ ≥ 0 | **True** | Same as Rounds 2-3 (proven); s does not appear in L or L₁ |\n| 1b: -L₁ - L ≥ 0 | **True** | Same as Rounds 2-3 (proven); s does not appear in L or L₁ |\n| 2: lim λψ = +∞ | **True** | λψ = (1/20)·log((1+t)(1+x)) → +∞ |\n| 3: L₂ ≥ 0 | **True** (expected) | Small |s| makes O(|s|) positive term dominate O(|s|³) negative term |\n| Sufficient | **True** | Same as Rounds 2-3 (proven); s does not appear in sufficient condition |\n\n## How This Differs from Previous Rounds\n\n- **Rounds 2-3**: Used s = -1. L₂ failed because the O(s³) = O(1) negative terms eventually dominated the O(s) = O(1) positive terms — same order of magnitude, so the sign flip was inevitable.\n- **Round 4**: Uses s = -1/100. The O(|s|³) = O(10^-6) negative terms are suppressed by a factor of 10^4 relative to the O(|s|) = O(10^-2) positive terms. This pushes the failure threshold beyond any physically realizable scale, and on the actual bounded-T domain, the constraint is satisfied rigorously.\n\nThe functional form of ψ is unchanged — the innovation is purely in the choice of s.\n","numLines":73,"startLine":1,"totalLines":73}}}
