 0.\n\nTherefore the sufficient condition numerator is ALWAYS POSITIVE. QED.\n\nf(0) = 120\nf(1) = 481\nf(0.001) = 751507/6250\n\n=== DETAILED L2 ANALYSIS ===\n\nMinimum of f(z) for z > 1: ≈ 205437\nR_crit = 205437\n(1+t)(1+x) must exceed 205437^10 ≈ 1.34e+53\n\nThis means L2 ≥ 0 for (1+t)(1+x) < ~10^53.\n\nHOWEVER, on the domain [0,∞) × [0,T] with T fixed:\n  In Case 2 (z > 1), we have (1+x) < c(1+t) ≤ c(1+T)\n  So (1+t)(1+x) < (1+T)*c(1+T) = c(1+T)^2\n  Hence R < (c(1+T)^2)^(1/10)\n  For any FIXED c, T, this is a finite number << 205437\n  => L2 > 0 in Case 2 for any fixed c, T.\n\nBUT: The problem asks for L2 >= 0 on [0,∞) × [0,∞), NOT [0,∞) × [0,T].\nIf both t and x can go to infinity independently, then R can grow without bound.\n\nLet's check: does the problem domain include t -> infinity?\nWe need to check the verify_engine conditions more carefully.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false}}
