{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_016ptTHq65zSokbGvKx1Xb4z","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Wave PINN — Automated Weight Function Search\n     2→\n     3→Automated search for a Carleman estimate weight function using a three-agent loop: **Search**, **Verify**, **Verdict**.\n     4→\n     5→Given the wave operator `□u = u_tt − c² u_xx`, the pipeline finds `ψ(x,t)`, `s`, `α`, `λ` such that a set of necessary and sufficient conditions hold on `[0,∞) × [0,T]`. The full problem is defined in `problem.tex`.\n     6→\n     7→## Prerequisites\n     8→\n     9→```bash\n    10→conda activate wave\n    11→```\n    12→\n    13→The `wave` conda environment must contain: `python 3.12+`, `numpy`, `sympy`, `jinja2`.\n    14→\n    15→To create it:\n    16→```bash\n    17→conda create -y -n wave python=3.12 numpy sympy jinja2\n    18→```\n    19→\n    20→Additionally, the following CLI tools must be on `$PATH`:\n    21→- `claude` — [Claude Code CLI](https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code)\n    22→- `jq` — JSON processor\n    23→\n    24→## Quick Start\n    25→\n    26→```bash\n    27→cd /home/cyanz/wave_PINN\n    28→./run.sh          # default 15 iterations\n    29→./run.sh 30       # custom max iterations\n    30→```\n    31→\n    32→## Pipeline Overview\n    33→\n    34→```\n    35→┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n    36→│                     run.sh (orchestrator)                 │\n    37→│                                                          │\n    38→│  for round in 1..max_iterations:                         │\n    39→│                                                          │\n    40→│    ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │\n    41→│    │ Step 0: Render prompts (Jinja2)                 │   │\n    42→│    │   render_prompts.py round_N project_root max    │   │\n    43→│    │   → candidates/round_N/prompt_search.md         │   │\n    44→│    │   → candidates/round_N/prompt_verify.md         │   │\n    45→│    │   → candidates/round_N/prompt_verdict.md        │   │\n    46→│    └─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │\n    47→│                          │                               │\n    48→│    ┌─────────────────────▼───────────────────────────┐   │\n    49→│    │ Step 1: Search Agent (Claude)                   │   │\n    50→│    │   reads: problem.tex, failed_approaches.md,     │   │\n    51→│    │          prev round's verification_report.md    │   │\n    52→│    │   writes: candidates/round_N/candidate.py       │   │\n    53→│    │           candidates/round_N/candidate_log.md   │   │\n    54→│    └─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │\n    55→│                          │                               │\n    56→│    ┌─────────────────────▼───────────────────────────┐   │\n    57→│    │ Step 2: Verify Agent (Claude)                   │   │\n    58→│    │   runs: python3 verify_engine.py candidate.py   │   │\n    59→│    │   writes: candidates/round_N/                   │   │\n    60→│    │           verification_report.md                │   │\n    61→│    └─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │\n    62→│                          │                               │\n    63→│    ┌─────────────────────▼───────────────────────────┐   │\n    64→│    │ Step 3: Verdict Agent (Claude)                  │   │\n    65→│    │   reads: verification_report.md                 │   │\n    66→│    │   outputs: DONE or CONTINUE                     │   │\n    67→│    └─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │\n    68→│                          │                               │\n    69→│         DONE ──► exit 0 (copy winning candidate)         │\n    70→│         CONTINUE ──► append to failed_approaches.md      │\n    71→│                       └──► next round                    │\n    72→└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n    73→```\n    74→\n    75→### Agent Details\n    76→\n    77→**Search Agent** — Proposes a candidate `ψ(x,t)` and parameters `(s, α, λ)`. It reads the problem definition, all previously failed approaches, and the previous round's verification report to avoid repeating mistakes and to fix specific failing conditions.\n    78→\n    79→**Verify Agent** — Runs `verify_engine.py` on the candidate (both human-readable and JSON modes), reads the output, and writes a structured verification report with per-condition status (True / False / unknown), symbolic expressions, numerical sampling results, and a failure analysis section diagnosing what went wrong.\n    80→\n    81→**Verdict Agent** — Reads the verification report and outputs exactly one word: `DONE` (all 4 conditions = True) or `CONTINUE` (any condition is False or unknown).\n    82→\n    83→## File Structure\n    84→\n    85→```\n    86→wave_PINN/\n    87→│\n    88→│  # ── Problem definition ──\n    89→├── problem.tex               # LaTeX: full problem statement with operator definitions\n    90→│\n    91→│  # ── Verification engine ──\n    92→├── verify_engine.py          # Symbolic + numerical verification of candidates\n    93→│                             #   CLI: python3 verify_engine.py <candidate.py> [--json]\n    94→│                             #   Checks all 4 conditions, returns structured results\n    95→├── verify.py                 # Original standalone verifier (kept for reference)\n    96→│\n    97→│  # ── Prompt templates (Jinja2) ──\n    98→├── prompts/\n    99→│   ├── search.md             # Search agent template — {{ problem_tex }}, {{ candidate_file }}, etc.\n   100→│   ├── verify.md             # Verify agent template — {{ project_root }}, {{ candidate_file }}, etc.\n   101→│   └── verdict.md            # Verdict agent template — {{ verification_report }}\n   102→│\n   103→│  # ── Orchestration ──\n   104→├── render_prompts.py         # Jinja2 renderer: fills templates with real paths per round\n   105→├── run.sh                    # Main loop: render → search → verify → verdict\n   106→│\n   107→│  # ── Candidates (generated at runtime) ──\n   108→└── candidates/\n   109→    ├── example_candidate.py  # Example candidate (for testing the engine)\n   110→    │\n   111→    │  # ── Created by run.sh per round ──\n   112→    ├── round_1/\n   113→    │   ├── prompt_search.md          # Rendered search prompt (all paths filled in)\n   114→    │   ├── prompt_verify.md          # Rendered verify prompt\n   115→    │   ├── prompt_verdict.md         # Rendered verdict prompt\n   116→    │   ├── candidate.py              # Written by search agent\n   117→    │   ├── candidate_log.md          # Written by search agent (reasoning)\n   118→    │   └── verification_report.md    # Written by verify agent\n   119→    ├── round_2/\n   120→    │   └── ...\n   121→    ├── round_N/\n   122→    │   └── ...\n   123→    │\n   124→    │  # ── Accumulated state ──\n   125→    ├── failed_approaches.md          # All failed candidates + why they failed\n   126→    ├── best_so_far.md                # Tracks best candidate across rounds\n   127→    ├── orchestrate_log.txt           # Full execution log\n   128→    ├── AUTO_RUN_STATUS.md            # Live status (current round, step, PID)\n   129→    │\n   130→    │  # ── Final output (on success) ──\n   131→    ├── winning_candidate.py          # Copy of the passing candidate\n   132→    └── winning_report.md             # Copy of the passing verification report\n   133→```\n   134→\n   135→## Input / Output Reference\n   136→\n   137→### Inputs (static, created before running)\n   138→\n   139→| File | Description |\n   140→|------|-------------|\n   141→| `problem.tex` | Full problem definition: operators □, ˜□, L, L₁, L₂, conditions, notation |\n   142→| `prompts/search.md` | Jinja2 template for the search agent |\n   143→| `prompts/verify.md` | Jinja2 template for the verify agent |\n   144→| `prompts/verdict.md` | Jinja2 template for the verdict agent |\n   145→| `verify_engine.py` | Verification engine (deterministic, not modified at runtime) |\n   146→\n   147→### Outputs (generated at runtime)\n   148→\n   149→| File | Created by | Description |\n   150→|------|-----------|-------------|\n   151→| `candidates/round_N/candidate.py` | Search agent | Defines `psi` and `subs_dict` |\n   152→| `candidates/round_N/candidate_log.md` | Search agent | Mathematical reasoning for the candidate |\n   153→| `candidates/round_N/verification_report.md` | Verify agent | Structured report with all condition statuses |\n   154→| `candidates/round_N/prompt_*.md` | `render_prompts.py` | Rendered prompts with real paths |\n   155→| `candidates/failed_approaches.md` | `run.sh` | Accumulates all failed candidates and failure analyses |\n   156→| `candidates/best_so_far.md` | `run.sh` | Tracks the best candidate found so far |\n   157→| `candidates/orchestrate_log.txt` | `run.sh` | Full timestamped execution log |\n   158→| `candidates/AUTO_RUN_STATUS.md` | `run.sh` | Live status for monitoring |\n   159→| `candidates/winning_candidate.py` | `run.sh` | Final passing candidate (on success) |\n   160→| `candidates/winning_report.md` | `run.sh` | Final passing verification report (on success) |\n   161→\n   162→## Candidate File Format\n   163→\n   164→Each candidate is a Python file defining two variables. Symbols (`x`, `t`, `c`, `x0`, `alpha`, `s`, `lam`, `sp`) are injected by `verify_engine.py` at load time — do not import anything.\n   165→\n   166→```python\n   167→# Description: quadratic in x with shifted center\n   168→psi = t - (x + x0)**2\n   169→\n   170→subs_dict = {\n   171→    alpha : 2,\n   172→    x0    : c / 2,\n   173→    s     : -2,       # must be negative\n   174→    lam   : -c**2,\n   175→}\n   176→```\n   177→\n   178→- `psi` — SymPy expression in `x`, `t`, and optionally auxiliary symbols like `x0`\n   179→- `subs_dict` — maps `alpha`, `s`, `lam` (and any auxiliary symbols) to values that may depend on `c`\n   180→\n   181→## Verification Conditions\n   182→\n   183→The engine checks four conditions (all must hold on `[0,∞) × [0,T]`):\n   184→\n   185→| # | Condition | Type |\n   186→|---|-----------|------|\n   187→| 1 | `L₁ψ ≤ Lψ ≤ −L₁ψ` | Necessary |\n   188→| 2 | `lim(x→+∞) λψ = +∞` | Necessary |\n   189→| 3 | `L₂ψ ≥ 0` | Necessary |\n   190→| 4 | `(L₁ψ)² − (Lψ)² ≥ c²(∂²ₓₜφ)²` | Sufficient |\n   191→\n   192→Each condition is checked symbolically (SymPy). If SymPy returns `unknown`, numerical sampling on a grid of `(x, t, c)` values is used as a fallback.\n   193→\n   194→## Running verify_engine.py Standalone\n   195→\n   196→```bash\n   197→conda activate wave\n   198→\n   199→# Human-readable output\n   200→python3 verify_engine.py candidates/example_candidate.py\n   201→\n   202→# JSON output\n   203→python3 verify_engine.py candidates/example_candidate.py --json\n   204→```\n   205→\n   206→## Termination\n   207→\n   208→- **Success** (`exit 0`): All 4 conditions return `True`. The winning candidate is copied to `candidates/winning_candidate.py`.\n   209→- **Failure** (`exit 1`): Max iterations reached without finding a valid candidate. See `candidates/failed_approaches.md` for all attempts and `candidates/best_so_far.md` for the closest candidate.\n   210→\n   211→## Monitoring a Running Search\n   212→\n   213→While `run.sh` is running:\n   214→\n   215→```bash\n   216→# Live status\n   217→cat candidates/AUTO_RUN_STATUS.md\n   218→\n   219→# Follow the log\n   220→tail -f candidates/orchestrate_log.txt\n   221→\n   222→# Check a specific round's result\n   223→cat candidates/round_3/verification_report.md\n   224→```\n   225→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01QZ6hQ9tY4CjMrJKfwbfMxN","session_id":"a8c51315-e5dc-4015-8318-7588456c8083","uuid":"1bcc926f-b690-42d0-8e56-7d276fed297e"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01HuiA6odFAjCVFnhmUCLb2m","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→\\documentclass[11pt]{amsart}\n     2→\\usepackage{amsmath,amsthm,amssymb,bm}\n     3→\\usepackage{xcolor}\n     4→\\usepackage{indentfirst}\n     5→\\usepackage{graphicx}\n     6→\\usepackage{tikz}\n     7→\\usetikzlibrary{decorations.pathreplacing, decorations.pathmorphing, decorations.shapes}\n     8→\\usetikzlibrary{arrows,calc}\n     9→\\usepackage{mathrsfs,esint}\n    10→\\usepackage{comment}\n    11→\\usepackage{enumerate}\n    12→\\usepackage{hyperref}\n    13→\\hypersetup{\n    14→    colorlinks = true,\n    15→    linkcolor = red,\n    16→    anchorcolor = green!50!black,\n    17→    citecolor = green!50!black,\n    18→    filecolor = green!50!black,\n    19→    urlcolor = green!50!black\n    20→}\n    21→\\usepackage{cleveref}\n    22→\n    23→\\usepackage[top=1in, bottom=1in, left=1.25in, right=1.25in]{geometry}\n    24→\\usepackage{setspace}\n    25→\n    26→\\usepackage{float}\n    27→\n    28→\\usepackage{subcaption}\n    29→\\usepackage{pgfplots}\n    30→\\pgfplotsset{compat=newest, compat/show suggested version=false}\n    31→\\usepackage{tikz}\n    32→\\usetikzlibrary{intersections}\n    33→\n    34→\\usepackage{diagbox}\n    35→\\usepackage{algorithm, algorithmicx, algpseudocode}\n    36→\\numberwithin{algorithm}{section}\n    37→\\usepackage{tabularx}\n    38→\\usepackage{tcolorbox}\n    39→\n    40→\\usepackage[titletoc,toc,title]{appendix}\n    41→\\renewcommand\\appendixname{Appendix}\n    42→\n    43→\\newtheorem{thm}{Theorem}[section]\n    44→\\newtheorem{theorem}[thm]{Theorem}\n    45→\\newtheorem{proposition}[thm]{Proposition}\n    46→\\newtheorem{corollary}[thm]{Corollary}\n    47→\\newtheorem{lemma}[thm]{Lemma}\n    48→\\newtheorem{remark}[thm]{\\textit{Remark}}\n    49→\\newtheorem{definition}[thm]{Definition}\n    50→\\newtheorem{assumption}[thm]{Assumption}\n    51→\\newtheorem{example}[thm]{Example}\n    52→\n    53→\\newcommand{\\titleShort}{Wave PINNs}\n    54→\\newcommand{\\titleLong}{Precise Goal and Verification Conditions for the Weight Construction}\n    55→\n    56→\\title[\\titleShort]{\\titleLong}\n    57→\n    58→\\thanks{}\n    59→\n    60→\\author{Author}\n    61→% \\address{Address}\n    62→% \\email{Email}\n    63→\n    64→\\date{}\n    65→\n    66→% \\renewcommand \\theequation {\\arabic{section}.\\arabic{equation}}\n    67→\\numberwithin{equation}{section}\n    68→\n    69→\\begin{document}\n    70→% \\maketitle\n    71→\n    72→%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%\n    73→\\section{Precise Goal}\n    74→\\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=green!5, colframe=green!75!blue, title=Goal with necessary conditions]\n    75→For given arbitrary wave speed $c > 0$ and terminal time $T > 0$, find\n    76→\\begin{equation*}\n    77→    \\left\\{\n    78→    \\begin{aligned}\n    79→        &\\psi(x, t) && \\text{function of $x$ and $t$},\\\\\n    80→        &s          && \\text{negative constant},\\\\\n    81→        &\\alpha     && \\text{constant},\\\\\n    82→        &\\lambda    && \\text{constant},\n    83→    \\end{aligned}\n    84→    \\right.\n    85→\\end{equation*}\n    86→such that the following necessary conditions are satisfied for all $(x, t) \\in [0, \\infty) \\times [0, T]$\n    87→\\begin{equation*}\n    88→    \\left\\{\n    89→    \\begin{aligned}\n    90→        &L_{1} \\psi < L \\psi < - L_{1} \\psi,\\\\\n    91→        &\\lim_{x \\to + \\infty} \\lambda \\psi = + \\infty,\\\\\n    92→        &L_{2} \\psi > 0.\n    93→    \\end{aligned}\n    94→    \\right.\n    95→\\end{equation*}\n    96→\\end{tcolorbox}\n    97→\n    98→\\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=orange!5, colframe=orange!80!blue, title=Sufficient condition]\n    99→The final verification concerns the sufficient condition for all $(x, t) \\in [0, \\infty) \\times [0, T]$\n   100→\\begin{equation*}\n   101→    (L_{1} \\psi)^{2} - (L \\psi)^{2}\n   102→    \\geq\n   103→    c^{2} (\\partial_{x, t}^{2} \\varphi)^{2}.\n   104→\\end{equation*}\n   105→\\end{tcolorbox}\n   106→\n   107→\\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=blue!5, colframe=blue!70, title=Definitions and notations]\n   108→\\begin{enumerate}\n   109→    \\item $\\varphi$: function of $x$ and $t$,\n   110→    \\begin{equation*}\n   111→        \\varphi\n   112→        =\n   113→        \\exp(\\lambda \\psi).\n   114→    \\end{equation*}\n   115→\n   116→    \\item $\\square$: operator acting on functions of $x$ and $t$,\n   117→    \\begin{equation*}\n   118→        \\square u\n   119→        =\n   120→        \\partial_{t}^{2} u - c^{2} \\partial_{x}^{2} u.\n   121→    \\end{equation*}\n   122→\n   123→    \\item $\\widetilde{\\square}$: operator acting on functions of $x$ and $t$,\n   124→    \\begin{equation*}\n   125→        \\widetilde{\\square} u\n   126→        =\n   127→        |\\partial_{t} u|^{2} - c^{2}|\\partial_{x} u|^{2}.\n   128→    \\end{equation*}\n   129→\n   130→    \\item $L$: operator acting on $\\psi$,\n   131→    \\begin{equation*}\n   132→        L \\psi\n   133→        =\n   134→        \\lambda^{2} \\varphi (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi)\n   135→        -\n   136→        (\\alpha - 1) \\lambda \\varphi (\\square \\psi).\n   137→    \\end{equation*}\n   138→\n   139→    \\item $L_{1}$: operator acting on $\\psi$,\n   140→    \\begin{equation*}\n   141→        \\begin{aligned}\n   142→            L_{1} \\psi\n   143→            &=\n   144→            c^{2} \\partial_{x}^{2} \\varphi\n   145→            +\n   146→            \\partial_{t}^{2} \\varphi\\\\\n   147→            &=\n   148→            \\lambda^{2} \\varphi\n   149→            \\left (\n   150→                (\\partial_{t} \\psi)^{2}\n   151→                +\n   152→                c^{2} (\\partial_{x} \\psi)^{2}\n   153→            \\right )\n   154→            +\n   155→            \\lambda \\varphi\n   156→            \\left (\n   157→                \\partial_{t}^{2} \\psi\n   158→                +\n   159→                c^{2} \\partial_{x}^{2} \\psi\n   160→            \\right ).\n   161→        \\end{aligned}\n   162→    \\end{equation*}\n   163→\n   164→    \\item $L_{2}$: operator acting on $\\psi$,\n   165→    \\begin{equation*}\n   166→    \\begin{aligned}\n   167→        L_{2}\n   168→        &=\n   169→        \\frac{1}{2}\n   170→        \\Big (\n   171→            (\\alpha - 1) s \\lambda (\\square (\\varphi (\\square \\psi)))\n   172→            -\n   173→            s \\lambda^{2} (\\square (\\varphi (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi)))\n   174→        \\Big )\\\\\n   175→        &+\n   176→        \\Big (\n   177→            s^{3} \\lambda^{3} (\\alpha - 1) \\varphi^{3} (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi) (\\square \\psi)\n   178→            -\n   179→            s^{3} \\lambda^{4} \\varphi^{3} (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi)^{2}\\\\\n   180→            &\\qquad+\n   181→            s^{3} \\lambda^{3}\n   182→            \\left (\n   183→                \\partial_{t} (\\varphi^{3} (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi) (\\partial_{t} \\psi))\n   184→                +\n   185→                c^{2} \\partial_{x}(\\varphi^{3} (\\widetilde{\\square} \\psi) (\\partial_{x} \\psi))\n   186→            \\right )\n   187→        \\Big ).\n   188→    \\end{aligned}\n   189→    \\end{equation*}\n   190→\\end{enumerate}\n   191→\\end{tcolorbox}\n   192→Note that $\\partial_{x}^{2} u = \\partial_{x} (\\partial_{x} u) = u_{x x}$.\n   193→This problem comes from the Carleman estimate for the wave operator $\\square$.\n   194→$\\psi, s, \\alpha, \\lambda$ could be dependent on $c$ and $T$.\n   195→If multiple $\\psi$ are possible, find the smoothest one.\n   196→\n   197→\\end{document}\n   198→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01QZ6hQ9tY4CjMrJKfwbfMxN","session_id":"a8c51315-e5dc-4015-8318-7588456c8083","uuid":"8ac87721-a950-4cf9-b729-46674846d41e"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01QZ6hQ9tY4CjMrJKfwbfMxN","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! I have found all the key information about the problem domain. Let me compile my findings:\n\n## Problem Domain Specification - Summary Report\n\nBased on my search through `/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN`, I have identified **the exact domain specification** for the problem:\n\n### **Domain: [0,∞) × [0,T]**\n\nThe problem domain is explicitly specified as **[0,∞) × [0,T]** (semi-infinite in space, finite in time).\n\n#### Evidence from multiple sources:\n\n1. **problem.tex** (Lines 75-86):\n   - \"For given arbitrary wave speed c > 0 and terminal time T > 0, find...\"\n   - \"such that the following necessary conditions are satisfied for all (x, t) ∈ [0, ∞) × [0, T]\"\n   - This is repeated for the sufficient condition as well (Line 99)\n\n2. **README.md** (Line 5):\n   - \"The pipeline finds ψ(x,t), s, α, λ such that a set of necessary and sufficient conditions hold on [0,∞) × [0,T].\"\n\n3. **prompts/search.md** (Line 13):\n   - \"For arbitrary c > 0 and T > 0, find ψ(x,t), s (negative constant), α, λ such that on [0,∞) × [0,T]:\"\n   - The markdown explicitly lists the necessary conditions that must hold on this bounded-time domain\n\n### Key Observations from Verification Reports:\n\n**Round 1 Verification Report** (verification_report.md):\n- Tested candidate with domain x ∈ [0, 200], t ∈ [0, 100]\n- Found failures at large t values (t ≥ 50 for c=1)\n- Notes that the engine's default sampling (x ∈ [0,50], t ∈ [0,10]) was \"too small to detect failures that occur at t > 28 (for c=2) or t > 57 (for c=1)\"\n\n**Round 2 Verification Report** (verification_report.md):\n- Extended testing to even larger domains: t up to 2.31×10⁷\n- L₂ condition fails at extremely large (1+t)(1+x) values\n- Critical threshold: R = ((1+t)(1+x))^(1/10) > 20.54, requiring (1+t)(1+x) > 1.34×10¹³\n\n### Domain Constraint Details:\n\nThe domain specification means:\n- **Spatial domain**: x ranges from 0 to infinity\n- **Time domain**: t ranges from 0 to a fixed terminal time T (arbitrarily chosen)\n- The conditions must hold **everywhere** in this semi-infinite domain\n- Both c > 0 and T > 0 are arbitrary (the solution should work for any positive values)\n\n### Files Containing Domain Information:\n\n- **/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/problem.tex** (lines 75-105)\n- **/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/README.md** (line 5)\n- **/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/prompts/search.md** (line 13)\n- **/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/candidates/round_1/verification_report.md** (implicit validation of domain)\n- **/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/candidates/round_2/verification_report.md** (implicit validation of domain)"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ab8f009 (for resuming to continue this agent's work if needed)"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a8c51315-e5dc-4015-8318-7588456c8083","uuid":"ff20beaa-6878-4fb7-825b-11e2bd10460d","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Search in /local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN for any files that specify the problem domain - whether the conditions need to hold on [0,∞)×[0,∞) or [0,∞)×[0,T] for fixed T. Look at files like prompts/search.md, any README, or any problem specification files. Also check previous verification reports in round_1 and round_2 directories for clues about the domain.","agentId":"ab8f009","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! I have found all the key information about the problem domain. Let me compile my findings:\n\n## Problem Domain Specification - Summary Report\n\nBased on my search through `/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN`, I have identified **the exact domain specification** for the problem:\n\n### **Domain: [0,∞) × [0,T]**\n\nThe problem domain is explicitly specified as **[0,∞) × [0,T]** (semi-infinite in space, finite in time).\n\n#### Evidence from multiple sources:\n\n1. **problem.tex** (Lines 75-86):\n   - \"For given arbitrary wave speed c > 0 and terminal time T > 0, find...\"\n   - \"such that the following necessary conditions are satisfied for all (x, t) ∈ [0, ∞) × [0, T]\"\n   - This is repeated for the sufficient condition as well (Line 99)\n\n2. **README.md** (Line 5):\n   - \"The pipeline finds ψ(x,t), s, α, λ such that a set of necessary and sufficient conditions hold on [0,∞) × [0,T].\"\n\n3. **prompts/search.md** (Line 13):\n   - \"For arbitrary c > 0 and T > 0, find ψ(x,t), s (negative constant), α, λ such that on [0,∞) × [0,T]:\"\n   - The markdown explicitly lists the necessary conditions that must hold on this bounded-time domain\n\n### Key Observations from Verification Reports:\n\n**Round 1 Verification Report** (verification_report.md):\n- Tested candidate with domain x ∈ [0, 200], t ∈ [0, 100]\n- Found failures at large t values (t ≥ 50 for c=1)\n- Notes that the engine's default sampling (x ∈ [0,50], t ∈ [0,10]) was \"too small to detect failures that occur at t > 28 (for c=2) or t > 57 (for c=1)\"\n\n**Round 2 Verification Report** (verification_report.md):\n- Extended testing to even larger domains: t up to 2.31×10⁷\n- L₂ condition fails at extremely large (1+t)(1+x) values\n- Critical threshold: R = ((1+t)(1+x))^(1/10) > 20.54, requiring (1+t)(1+x) > 1.34×10¹³\n\n### Domain Constraint Details:\n\nThe domain specification means:\n- **Spatial domain**: x ranges from 0 to infinity\n- **Time domain**: t ranges from 0 to a fixed terminal time T (arbitrarily chosen)\n- The conditions must hold **everywhere** in this semi-infinite domain\n- Both c > 0 and T > 0 are arbitrary (the solution should work for any positive values)\n\n### Files Containing Domain Information:\n\n- **/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/problem.tex** (lines 75-105)\n- **/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/README.md** (line 5)\n- **/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/prompts/search.md** (line 13)\n- **/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/candidates/round_1/verification_report.md** (implicit validation of domain)\n- **/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/candidates/round_2/verification_report.md** (implicit validation of domain)"}],"totalDurationMs":20823,"totalTokens":101956,"totalToolUseCount":8,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7033,"cache_read_input_tokens":94915,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7033,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1}}}
