{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_015uU6NF3o3Ms5kQqUuhZCBs","type":"tool_result","content":"    70→% \\maketitle\n    71→\n    72→%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%\n    73→\\section{Precise Goal}\n    74→\\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=green!5, colframe=green!75!blue, title=Goal with necessary conditions]\n    75→For given arbitrary wave speed $c > 0$ and terminal time $T > 0$, find\n    76→\\begin{equation*}\n    77→    \\left\\{\n    78→    \\begin{aligned}\n    79→        &\\psi(x, t) && \\text{function of $x$ and $t$},\\\\\n    80→        &s          && \\text{negative constant},\\\\\n    81→        &\\alpha     && \\text{constant},\\\\\n    82→        &\\lambda    && \\text{constant},\n    83→    \\end{aligned}\n    84→    \\right.\n    85→\\end{equation*}\n    86→such that the following necessary conditions are satisfied for all $(x, t) \\in [0, \\infty) \\times [0, T]$\n    87→\\begin{equation*}\n    88→    \\left\\{\n    89→    \\begin{aligned}\n    90→        &L_{1} \\psi < L \\psi < - L_{1} \\psi,\\\\\n    91→        &\\lim_{x \\to + \\infty} \\lambda \\psi = + \\infty,\\\\\n    92→        &L_{2} \\psi > 0.\n    93→    \\end{aligned}\n    94→    \\right.\n    95→\\end{equation*}\n    96→\\end{tcolorbox}\n    97→\n    98→\\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=orange!5, colframe=orange!80!blue, title=Sufficient condition]\n    99→The final verification concerns the sufficient condition for all $(x, t) \\in [0, \\infty) \\times [0, T]$\n   100→\\begin{equation*}\n   101→    (L_{1} \\psi)^{2} - (L \\psi)^{2}\n   102→    \\geq\n   103→    c^{2} (\\partial_{x, t}^{2} \\varphi)^{2}.\n   104→\\end{equation*}\n   105→\\end{tcolorbox}\n   106→\n   107→\\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=blue!5, colframe=blue!70, title=Definitions and notations]\n   108→\\begin{enumerate}\n   109→    \\item $\\varphi$: function of $x$ and $t$,\n   110→    \\begin{equation*}\n   111→        \\varphi\n   112→        =\n   113→        \\exp(\\lambda \\psi).\n   114→    \\end{equation*}\n   115→\n   116→    \\item $\\square$: operator acting on functions of $x$ and $t$,\n   117→    \\begin{equation*}\n   118→        \\square u\n   119→        =\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a8c51315-e5dc-4015-8318-7588456c8083","uuid":"84517a5f-b5bc-4492-97e1-e8647d746550","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/problem.tex","content":"% \\maketitle\n\n%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%\n\\section{Precise Goal}\n\\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=green!5, colframe=green!75!blue, title=Goal with necessary conditions]\nFor given arbitrary wave speed $c > 0$ and terminal time $T > 0$, find\n\\begin{equation*}\n    \\left\\{\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &\\psi(x, t) && \\text{function of $x$ and $t$},\\\\\n        &s          && \\text{negative constant},\\\\\n        &\\alpha     && \\text{constant},\\\\\n        &\\lambda    && \\text{constant},\n    \\end{aligned}\n    \\right.\n\\end{equation*}\nsuch that the following necessary conditions are satisfied for all $(x, t) \\in [0, \\infty) \\times [0, T]$\n\\begin{equation*}\n    \\left\\{\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        &L_{1} \\psi < L \\psi < - L_{1} \\psi,\\\\\n        &\\lim_{x \\to + \\infty} \\lambda \\psi = + \\infty,\\\\\n        &L_{2} \\psi > 0.\n    \\end{aligned}\n    \\right.\n\\end{equation*}\n\\end{tcolorbox}\n\n\\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=orange!5, colframe=orange!80!blue, title=Sufficient condition]\nThe final verification concerns the sufficient condition for all $(x, t) \\in [0, \\infty) \\times [0, T]$\n\\begin{equation*}\n    (L_{1} \\psi)^{2} - (L \\psi)^{2}\n    \\geq\n    c^{2} (\\partial_{x, t}^{2} \\varphi)^{2}.\n\\end{equation*}\n\\end{tcolorbox}\n\n\\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=blue!5, colframe=blue!70, title=Definitions and notations]\n\\begin{enumerate}\n    \\item $\\varphi$: function of $x$ and $t$,\n    \\begin{equation*}\n        \\varphi\n        =\n        \\exp(\\lambda \\psi).\n    \\end{equation*}\n\n    \\item $\\square$: operator acting on functions of $x$ and $t$,\n    \\begin{equation*}\n        \\square u\n        =","numLines":50,"startLine":70,"totalLines":198}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_011Sdv9qLuRsu1Vh7F7HJX6x","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Search Agent: Propose a Candidate Weight Function\n     2→\n     3→## Environment Setup\n     4→Before running any Python command, always activate the conda environment first:\n     5→```bash\n     6→conda activate wave\n     7→```\n     8→\n     9→## Your Role\n    10→You are a mathematical search agent. Your task is to propose a candidate weight function ψ(x, t) and parameters (s, α, λ) for a Carleman estimate for the wave operator.\n    11→\n    12→## Problem Summary\n    13→For arbitrary c > 0 and T > 0, find ψ(x,t), s (negative constant), α, λ such that on [0,∞) × [0,T]:\n    14→\n    15→**Necessary conditions:**\n    16→1. L₁ψ ≤ Lψ ≤ −L₁ψ  (equivalently: L−L₁ ≥ 0 AND −L₁−L ≥ 0; this forces L₁ψ ≤ 0)\n    17→2. lim(x→+∞) λψ = +∞\n    18→3. L₂ψ ≥ 0\n    19→\n    20→**Sufficient condition:**\n    21→4. (L₁ψ)² − (Lψ)² ≥ c²(∂²ₓₜφ)²\n    22→\n    23→where φ = exp(λψ), and the operators are:\n    24→- □u = u_tt − c² u_xx\n    25→- ˜□u = (u_t)² − c²(u_x)²\n    26→- Lψ = λ²φ(˜□ψ) − (α−1)λφ(□ψ)\n    27→- L₁ψ = c²φ_xx + φ_tt = λ²φ((ψ_t)² + c²(ψ_x)²) + λφ(ψ_tt + c²ψ_xx)\n    28→- L₂ is a lengthy expression (see problem.tex for full definition)\n    29→\n    30→ψ, s, α, λ may depend on c and T. If multiple ψ work, prefer the smoothest one.\n    31→\n    32→## Key Mathematical Insights for the Search\n    33→\n    34→### Condition 1 structure\n    35→L₁ψ ≤ Lψ ≤ −L₁ψ implies L₁ψ ≤ 0 everywhere. Since:\n    36→  L₁ψ = λ²φ((ψ_t)² + c²(ψ_x)²) + λφ(ψ_tt + c²ψ_xx)\n    37→\n    38→and φ = exp(λψ) > 0, λ² > 0, this means:\n    39→  λ²((ψ_t)² + c²(ψ_x)²) + λ(ψ_tt + c²ψ_xx) ≤ 0\n    40→\n    41→So λ(ψ_tt + c²ψ_xx) must be negative enough to dominate the gradient-squared term.\n    42→\n    43→### Condition 2 structure\n    44→lim(x→+∞) λψ = +∞ means ψ must grow (or decay) as x→∞ in a direction where λψ → +∞.\n    45→If ψ ~ −x² (negative quadratic), then λ < 0 gives λψ ~ +x² → +∞. ✓\n    46→\n    47→### Sufficient condition structure\n    48→(L₁ψ)² − (Lψ)² ≥ c²(φ_xt)². Note L₁² − L² = (L₁+L)(L₁−L). Condition 1 says:\n    49→  L − L₁ ≥ 0, so L₁ − L ≤ 0\n    50→  −L₁ − L ≥ 0, so L₁ + L ≤ 0\n    51→So (L₁+L)(L₁−L) = product of two non-positive terms = non-negative ≥ 0. The question is whether it's large enough to dominate c²(φ_xt)².\n    52→\n    53→### Common ansatz families to try\n    54→1. **Quadratic in x**: ψ = t − (x + x₀)² or ψ = a·t − b·(x + x₀)²\n    55→2. **Separated variables**: ψ = f(t) − g(x) where g grows at infinity\n    56→3. **Linear + quadratic**: ψ = a·t + b·x − d·x²\n    57→4. **Rational or more complex**: if simpler forms fail\n    58→\n    59→## Diversity Requirement\n    60→\n    61→**Do NOT get stuck on one ansatz family.** If the failed approaches log shows that a particular functional form (e.g. quadratic in x, or separated variables) has failed multiple times in a row, you should consider switch to a fundamentally different construction. Tweaking parameters on a repeatedly failing structure is unlikely to work — change the structure itself.\n    62→\n    63→Strategies for diversifying:\n    64→- If polynomial forms keep failing, try exponential, logarithmic, or rational forms.\n    65→- If separated-variable forms f(t) − g(x) keep failing, try coupled forms where x and t interact (e.g. ψ = h(x − ct), ψ = x·f(t) − g(x), or ψ involving x·t terms).\n    66→- If simple low-degree forms keep failing, try higher-degree or piecewise-smooth constructions.\n    67→- Revisit the failure analyses: identify which condition is the persistent bottleneck and design ψ specifically to satisfy that condition first, then check the others.\n    68→\n    69→## Instructions\n    70→\n    71→1. **Read the problem file** at `{{ problem_tex }}` to get the full operator definitions.\n    72→2. **Read the failed approaches file** at `{{ failed_approaches }}` to understand what has been tried before and WHY it failed. Do NOT repeat a failed approach unless you have a specific reason to believe a different parameter choice fixes it. Pay attention to how many times a given ansatz family has been tried — if it has failed 2+ times, move on to a different family.\n    73→{% if prev_report %}\n    74→3. **Read the previous round's verification report** at `{{ prev_report }}` to understand which conditions failed and the diagnostic expressions.\n    75→{% else %}\n    76→3. This is round 1 — there is no previous verification report.\n    77→{% endif %}\n    78→4. **Think carefully** about what mathematical structure ψ needs to satisfy all four conditions simultaneously. Reason about signs, growth rates, and dominant terms.\n    79→5. **Write the candidate file** to `{{ candidate_file }}`. The file must define:\n    80→   - `psi`: a SymPy expression in terms of `x, t` and optionally auxiliary symbols like `x0`\n    81→   - `subs_dict`: a dict mapping `alpha`, `s`, `lam` (and any auxiliary symbols) to concrete values (which may depend on `c`)\n    82→6. **Write a reasoning log** to `{{ candidate_log }}` explaining:\n    83→   - What mathematical reasoning led to this choice\n    84→   - What you expect each condition's status to be and why\n    85→   - How this differs from previous failed attempts (if any)\n    86→\n    87→## Candidate File Format\n    88→The candidate file will be loaded by verify_engine.py. Symbols `x, t, c, x0, alpha, s, lam, sp` are injected at load time. Example:\n    89→\n    90→```python\n    91→# Description: <brief description of the ansatz>\n    92→psi = t - (x + x0)**2\n    93→\n    94→subs_dict = {\n    95→    alpha : 2,\n    96→    x0    : c / 2,\n    97→    s     : -2,\n    98→    lam   : -c**2,\n    99→}\n   100→```\n   101→\n   102→IMPORTANT:\n   103→- `s` MUST be negative.\n   104→- `psi` must be a valid SymPy expression.\n   105→- `subs_dict` values may depend on `c` (which is a positive SymPy symbol).\n   106→- Keep ψ as smooth and simple as possible.\n   107→- Do NOT import anything in the candidate file; all symbols are pre-injected.\n   108→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a8c51315-e5dc-4015-8318-7588456c8083","uuid":"f8379893-c759-4779-9cb4-b3e1c7a9c5e1","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/local/home/cyanz/wave_PINN/prompts/search.md","content":"# Search Agent: Propose a Candidate Weight Function\n\n## Environment Setup\nBefore running any Python command, always activate the conda environment first:\n```bash\nconda activate wave\n```\n\n## Your Role\nYou are a mathematical search agent. Your task is to propose a candidate weight function ψ(x, t) and parameters (s, α, λ) for a Carleman estimate for the wave operator.\n\n## Problem Summary\nFor arbitrary c > 0 and T > 0, find ψ(x,t), s (negative constant), α, λ such that on [0,∞) × [0,T]:\n\n**Necessary conditions:**\n1. L₁ψ ≤ Lψ ≤ −L₁ψ  (equivalently: L−L₁ ≥ 0 AND −L₁−L ≥ 0; this forces L₁ψ ≤ 0)\n2. lim(x→+∞) λψ = +∞\n3. L₂ψ ≥ 0\n\n**Sufficient condition:**\n4. (L₁ψ)² − (Lψ)² ≥ c²(∂²ₓₜφ)²\n\nwhere φ = exp(λψ), and the operators are:\n- □u = u_tt − c² u_xx\n- ˜□u = (u_t)² − c²(u_x)²\n- Lψ = λ²φ(˜□ψ) − (α−1)λφ(□ψ)\n- L₁ψ = c²φ_xx + φ_tt = λ²φ((ψ_t)² + c²(ψ_x)²) + λφ(ψ_tt + c²ψ_xx)\n- L₂ is a lengthy expression (see problem.tex for full definition)\n\nψ, s, α, λ may depend on c and T. If multiple ψ work, prefer the smoothest one.\n\n## Key Mathematical Insights for the Search\n\n### Condition 1 structure\nL₁ψ ≤ Lψ ≤ −L₁ψ implies L₁ψ ≤ 0 everywhere. Since:\n  L₁ψ = λ²φ((ψ_t)² + c²(ψ_x)²) + λφ(ψ_tt + c²ψ_xx)\n\nand φ = exp(λψ) > 0, λ² > 0, this means:\n  λ²((ψ_t)² + c²(ψ_x)²) + λ(ψ_tt + c²ψ_xx) ≤ 0\n\nSo λ(ψ_tt + c²ψ_xx) must be negative enough to dominate the gradient-squared term.\n\n### Condition 2 structure\nlim(x→+∞) λψ = +∞ means ψ must grow (or decay) as x→∞ in a direction where λψ → +∞.\nIf ψ ~ −x² (negative quadratic), then λ < 0 gives λψ ~ +x² → +∞. ✓\n\n### Sufficient condition structure\n(L₁ψ)² − (Lψ)² ≥ c²(φ_xt)². Note L₁² − L² = (L₁+L)(L₁−L). Condition 1 says:\n  L − L₁ ≥ 0, so L₁ − L ≤ 0\n  −L₁ − L ≥ 0, so L₁ + L ≤ 0\nSo (L₁+L)(L₁−L) = product of two non-positive terms = non-negative ≥ 0. The question is whether it's large enough to dominate c²(φ_xt)².\n\n### Common ansatz families to try\n1. **Quadratic in x**: ψ = t − (x + x₀)² or ψ = a·t − b·(x + x₀)²\n2. **Separated variables**: ψ = f(t) − g(x) where g grows at infinity\n3. **Linear + quadratic**: ψ = a·t + b·x − d·x²\n4. **Rational or more complex**: if simpler forms fail\n\n## Diversity Requirement\n\n**Do NOT get stuck on one ansatz family.** If the failed approaches log shows that a particular functional form (e.g. quadratic in x, or separated variables) has failed multiple times in a row, you should consider switch to a fundamentally different construction. Tweaking parameters on a repeatedly failing structure is unlikely to work — change the structure itself.\n\nStrategies for diversifying:\n- If polynomial forms keep failing, try exponential, logarithmic, or rational forms.\n- If separated-variable forms f(t) − g(x) keep failing, try coupled forms where x and t interact (e.g. ψ = h(x − ct), ψ = x·f(t) − g(x), or ψ involving x·t terms).\n- If simple low-degree forms keep failing, try higher-degree or piecewise-smooth constructions.\n- Revisit the failure analyses: identify which condition is the persistent bottleneck and design ψ specifically to satisfy that condition first, then check the others.\n\n## Instructions\n\n1. **Read the problem file** at `{{ problem_tex }}` to get the full operator definitions.\n2. **Read the failed approaches file** at `{{ failed_approaches }}` to understand what has been tried before and WHY it failed. Do NOT repeat a failed approach unless you have a specific reason to believe a different parameter choice fixes it. Pay attention to how many times a given ansatz family has been tried — if it has failed 2+ times, move on to a different family.\n{% if prev_report %}\n3. **Read the previous round's verification report** at `{{ prev_report }}` to understand which conditions failed and the diagnostic expressions.\n{% else %}\n3. This is round 1 — there is no previous verification report.\n{% endif %}\n4. **Think carefully** about what mathematical structure ψ needs to satisfy all four conditions simultaneously. Reason about signs, growth rates, and dominant terms.\n5. **Write the candidate file** to `{{ candidate_file }}`. The file must define:\n   - `psi`: a SymPy expression in terms of `x, t` and optionally auxiliary symbols like `x0`\n   - `subs_dict`: a dict mapping `alpha`, `s`, `lam` (and any auxiliary symbols) to concrete values (which may depend on `c`)\n6. **Write a reasoning log** to `{{ candidate_log }}` explaining:\n   - What mathematical reasoning led to this choice\n   - What you expect each condition's status to be and why\n   - How this differs from previous failed attempts (if any)\n\n## Candidate File Format\nThe candidate file will be loaded by verify_engine.py. Symbols `x, t, c, x0, alpha, s, lam, sp` are injected at load time. Example:\n\n```python\n# Description: <brief description of the ansatz>\npsi = t - (x + x0)**2\n\nsubs_dict = {\n    alpha : 2,\n    x0    : c / 2,\n    s     : -2,\n    lam   : -c**2,\n}\n```\n\nIMPORTANT:\n- `s` MUST be negative.\n- `psi` must be a valid SymPy expression.\n- `subs_dict` values may depend on `c` (which is a positive SymPy symbol).\n- Keep ψ as smooth and simple as possible.\n- Do NOT import anything in the candidate file; all symbols are pre-injected.\n","numLines":108,"startLine":1,"totalLines":108}}}
